WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.980
NASA's new Arctic laser station tracks satellites with single photon precision from the northernmost site on Earth. This is Fascinating Frontiers, Episode 262. A faster satellite laser-ranging system began operations on October 12th at the Nai-Alison Geodetic Observatory in Svalbard, according to NASA. The instrument fires thousands of pulses per second and detects single photons, allowing more accurate orbit tracking than older systems that fired only tens of times per second. It is the first NASA next-generation

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:33.740
SLR station and the northernmost facility of its kind.

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:39.320
The remote Arctic location improves coverage of polar orbiting satellites such as ISAT-2

00:00:39.320 --> 00:00:43.600
and Grace-FO, which the station can observe on nearly every orbit.

00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:49.320
The system also supports the terrestrial reference frame used for GPS, sea level measurements,

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:50.680
and tectonic studies.

00:00:51.400 --> 00:00:56.040
Automation improvements were driven by the site's proximity to a bird sanctuary that restricts

00:00:56.040 --> 00:00:56.700
summer access.

00:00:56.700 --> 00:01:03.007
Researchers modeled how laser-driven solar sales behave once they reach 75% of the speed

00:01:03.007 --> 00:01:04.787
of light according to Science Daily.

00:01:05.267 --> 00:01:10.167
At that velocity, relativistic aberration concentrates diffusely scattered light in the

00:01:10.167 --> 00:01:13.747
forward direction, producing a recoil force that acts as drag.

00:01:14.327 --> 00:01:19.827
The study examined three momentum transfer mechanisms, incident light, specular reflection,

00:01:20.127 --> 00:01:20.987
and diffuse scattering.

00:01:21.707 --> 00:01:26.827
Incident light remains the strongest contributor, yet all three lose effectiveness as the incoming

00:01:26.827 --> 00:01:29.427
laser beam red shifts from the sales perspective.

00:01:29.847 --> 00:01:35.127
The finding applies to concepts such as breakthrough starshot that aim to accelerate small probes

00:01:35.127 --> 00:01:37.027
to a substantial fraction of light speed.

00:01:37.961 --> 00:01:44.821
Analysis of 257 black hole mergers detected by LIGO, Virgo, and Cagra revealed that objects

00:01:44.821 --> 00:01:49.481
near ten solar masses appear in roughly three-quarters of events according to Science Daily.

00:01:50.141 --> 00:01:53.481
A clear shortage exists between twelve and sixteen solar masses.

00:01:53.481 --> 00:01:59.381
Black holes near ten solar masses also show more uniform spin rates than the overall population.

00:01:59.961 --> 00:02:04.821
The pattern matches predictions for stars that collapse directly into black holes without exploding.

00:02:05.421 --> 00:02:11.181
The study was led by Caltech physicist Katarina Chatzioanu with lead author Isaac Legred.

00:02:11.621 --> 00:02:19.949
The peasant simulation combines three supercomputers and an AI model that predicts gas evolution after supernovae according to Fizz.org.

00:02:19.949 --> 00:02:29.929
The AI replaces direct calculations that would otherwise require 100-year time steps, allowing the full galaxy run to advance in 100,000-year increments.

00:02:30.409 --> 00:02:41.289
The approach runs roughly 20 times faster than exhaustive computation. It was presented at the SC-2026 conference and developed by teams including the University of Tokyo and Reichen.

00:02:42.422 --> 00:02:49.842
Observations from the WM Keck Observatory show that the diffuse sodium cloud around Jupiter's moon Europa has stayed remarkably constant,

00:02:49.842 --> 00:02:53.482
despite the dynamic Jovian environment according to Fizz.org.

00:02:54.042 --> 00:02:59.042
The stability supports an oceanic source for the sodium rather than sporadic surface processes.

00:02:59.702 --> 00:03:04.702
Data span more than 20 years. The legacy survey of space and time has started operations

00:03:04.702 --> 00:03:11.442
at the Veracy Rubin Observatory according to astronomy. The 8.4 meter telescope with its 3.2

00:03:11.442 --> 00:03:17.562
gigapixel camera will capture about 1000 images each night. The survey follows more than 20 years

00:03:17.562 --> 00:03:18.762
of planning and construction.

00:03:19.402 --> 00:03:24.062
A new study in the astrophysical journal tracked roughly 200 jet-like plasma bursts

00:03:24.062 --> 00:03:28.242
called dynamic fibrils originating from sunspots, according to Universe Today.

00:03:29.002 --> 00:03:31.982
The jets are launched by sound waves generated in the spots.

00:03:32.542 --> 00:03:37.242
The observations add detail to explanations for why the corona is hundreds of times hotter

00:03:37.242 --> 00:03:38.302
than the sun's surface.

00:03:39.302 --> 00:03:44.362
Astronomers combine measurements from 37 earlier studies into a single catalog to examine what

00:03:44.362 --> 00:03:50.642
drives one-sided mass distributions in spiral galaxies, according to Fizz.org. Stanford researchers

00:03:50.642 --> 00:03:55.922
showed that the size and movement of individual sand grains on dunes can serve as direct indicators

00:03:55.922 --> 00:04:01.486
of wind velocity on planets with atmospheres according to universe today. On Earth, we judge

00:04:01.486 --> 00:04:06.626
wind by watching flags or feeling it on our faces. Yet on distant worlds, the only record

00:04:06.626 --> 00:04:12.106
often comes from patterns left in sand. Dunes form wherever loose grains are moved by wind

00:04:12.106 --> 00:04:17.006
or other flows, and their overall shape and orientation have long told researchers which

00:04:17.006 --> 00:04:21.846
way the wind blows. The new work shifts attention from the dunes themselves to the individual

00:04:21.846 --> 00:04:26.726
grains that build them. Granes of different sizes respond differently to the same wind.

00:04:27.166 --> 00:04:32.126
Smaller particles lift more easily and travel farther, while larger ones require stronger

00:04:32.126 --> 00:04:37.666
gusts. By measuring how far and how fast grains of known sizes move across a dune field,

00:04:37.666 --> 00:04:41.546
Researchers can back-calculate the wind speeds that produce those movements.

00:04:42.166 --> 00:04:47.226
The Stanford Analysis used laboratory experiments and numerical models to map grain trajectories

00:04:47.226 --> 00:04:51.926
under controlled winds, then showed that the same relationships appear in orbital images

00:04:51.926 --> 00:04:53.526
of dunes on Mars and Titan.

00:04:54.146 --> 00:04:59.206
Because the method relies only on visible grain motion and dune morphology, it can be applied

00:04:59.206 --> 00:05:01.346
wherever high-resolution images exist.

00:05:01.926 --> 00:05:07.446
What remains unknown is how well the calibration holds when atmospheric density, grain composition

00:05:07.446 --> 00:05:10.986
or electrostatic forces differ sharply from the tested cases.

00:05:11.586 --> 00:05:13.746
Direct wind measurements from future landers

00:05:13.746 --> 00:05:15.366
would provide the ground truth needed

00:05:15.366 --> 00:05:18.086
to refine the speed estimates across the solar system.

